WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.516
Stress is a normal response to demand, uncertainty, and consequence.

2
00:00:04.516 --> 00:00:09.936
In learning, some challenge can focus effort and reveal what needs improvement.

3
00:00:09.936 --> 00:00:14.452
But challenge becomes less productive when it turns into overload.

4
00:00:14.452 --> 00:00:24.840
If the learner is too overwhelmed to notice feedback, think clearly, or correct errors, the practice session may create strain without much learning.

5
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.904
Productive challenge stretches current ability without overwhelming the learner.

6
00:00:28.904 --> 00:00:34.324
It has a clear target, enough support, and a path for correction.

7
00:00:34.324 --> 00:00:42.002
Overload often looks different: rushing, tunnel vision, repeated errors, emotional shutdown, or inability to explain what changed.

8
00:00:42.002 --> 00:00:46.518
Good practice design adjusts the difficulty so effort remains useful.

9
00:00:46.518 --> 00:00:48.776
Recovery is part of adaptation.

10
00:00:48.776 --> 00:00:56.002
Breaks, transitions, pacing, and reasonable workload management help learners return with enough attention to practice again.

11
00:00:56.002 --> 00:00:58.712
This is not only about comfort.

12
00:00:58.712 --> 00:01:03.228
Sustainable improvement depends on balancing effort, rest, and system repair.

13
00:01:03.228 --> 00:01:10.906
Training that treats recovery as weakness may produce short bursts of intensity but poorer long-term consistency.

14
00:01:10.906 --> 00:01:13.164
This course stays non-clinical.

15
00:01:13.164 --> 00:01:22.648
Practical emotional regulation might include pausing before a difficult task, naming the next action, or stepping away briefly before reviewing feedback.

16
00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:28.068
Health, safety, or mental health concerns should be handled with qualified support.

17
00:01:28.068 --> 00:01:37.100
The responsible message is that learning systems should reduce unnecessary overload and make recovery a normal part of sustained performance.
